WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:08.080
Speaker 1: In the meantime, we continue to&nbsp;
see Joe Biden's approval rating go up and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:12.800
Republicans are furious. Many of them are leaving&nbsp;
me voicemails, which I'm not going to play because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:19.760
they're just very lame. But Republicans are&nbsp;
now saying Biden is as unpopular as ever, when,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.760 --> 00:00:26.960
of course, the truth is Joe Biden has seen one&nbsp;
of the most rapid increases in approval rating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:36.000
of any president in recent years. Now he's still&nbsp;
only at about 43%. And it's sort of a glass half&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:42.400
full, glass half empty sort of thing. You can&nbsp;
say, well, 43% means 15, 57% still disapprove.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.400 --> 00:00:46.640
But you can also look at the last several&nbsp;
presidencies and realize that actually 43%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:53.680
it's no incredible victory, but it's pretty&nbsp;
average in terms of where many presidents tend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.680 --> 00:00:59.120
to be during their presidencies. So taking&nbsp;
a look at the numbers now on the screen. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:04.960
see that Joe Biden had this sort of bottom&nbsp;
out at 37 and a half percent. Now, remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:08.720
FiveThirtyEight is looking at&nbsp;
an average of approval polls.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:16.560
So it's an it's it's based on more than one poll.&nbsp;
These are averages. The approval bottomed out at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:24.400
37 and a half percent in the second half of July.&nbsp;
And it's been a basically direct increase now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:32.880
to about 43%. So that's roughly an increase&nbsp;
of 15% in Joe Biden's approval rating. And I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.040
have to tell you, it sort of makes sense in&nbsp;
that lots of good things have taken place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:46.480
many of them associated with Joe Biden. There's&nbsp;
this crew. It's almost quaint sounding that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:51.760
a president does a bunch of stuff people like, and&nbsp;
then their approval rating goes up because we're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.760 --> 00:02:00.640
so used to this highly partizan, almost weaponized&nbsp;
approval rating situation where approval is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:06.720
more related to propaganda rather than&nbsp;
accomplishments. But we now have two jobs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:13.280
reports in a row that are better than expectations&nbsp;
under the Biden administration. We have inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.880
down last month. We are days away from the&nbsp;
next inflation number, and my expectation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:24.240
is that it's going to be down again. Significant&nbsp;
legislative accomplishments. Infrastructure. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.200
Pact Act. The Inflation Reduction Act. Student&nbsp;
loan forgiveness, which is very popular with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:33.120
Americans. And it's reflected now in the approval&nbsp;
rating. And there's other things as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:41.840
Gas prices also are continuing to experience&nbsp;
one of the most sustained and rapid declines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:51.760
in a very, very long time. And average gas prices&nbsp;
peaked at $5.02 in mid-June, and it has been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:58.000
declined. Decline, decline initially, very rapid,&nbsp;
then a little bit less rapid, but continuing down.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:07.680
Gas prices are now down to 3.73 from 502a&nbsp;
gallon. That is a 25 ish percent, 26% decline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:14.960
That is more than a quarter chopped off of gas&nbsp;
prices. Everybody's gas bill down by a quarter.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:21.520
This is a big, big deal. I don't even claim it has&nbsp;
anything to do with Joe Biden. I didn't believe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:26.320
Biden was responsible for the gas prices going&nbsp;
up. And I don't believe Joe Biden is responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:31.040
for the gas prices going down aside from a few&nbsp;
cents a gallon because of of of tapping the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:37.120
Strategic Petroleum Reserve. But I'm consistent.&nbsp;
I don't play the gas price games with presidents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.880
but we know that that's something many people&nbsp;
do look at when answering the question, do you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:48.640
approve of the job that the president is doing? So&nbsp;
we'll have new inflation data next week. I expect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:56.640
that it will be, again, a decline in inflation.&nbsp;
And Joe Biden's approval rating reflecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:04.640
what is overall a pretty damn good economy by all&nbsp;
standard metrics. And now the question becomes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:11.200
what does it do to Democratic chances in the fall?&nbsp;
And the numbers continue to improve a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.920
at a time. A little bit at a time. We will delve&nbsp;
into those numbers in more detail tomorrow.

